window.docContents[74].resolve({"/rules/RG1003/#RG1003-established":{"contents":"Every axiom in the transitive set of each owned declaration is either a standard logical axiom or rejected by a more specific rule.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* Anything about the unowned dependency beyond its axioms; imported declarations are a declared trust boundary, identified by the exact dependency state (RG2001).\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG1003: Unknown transitive axioms are forbidden","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG1003/#RG1003-established"},"/rules/RG2002/#RG2002-established":{"contents":"The manifest has the exact schema and classifies every root-package library and executable exactly once.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the chosen claims and exclusions are appropriate for the project; that is reviewed with each rationale.* Exclusions do not permit a claimed module to import an excluded one (RG2004).\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits* R-INVARIANT: the intended invariants, write paths and callers are all covered by proof-bearing interfaces\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG2002: Configuration must classify the complete Lake surface","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG2002/#RG2002-established"},"/rules/RG3001/#RG3001-established":{"contents":"Every path in each owned executable root's conservative closure was resolved and classified; an unresolved path is incomplete and never accepted.\n\nIt does not establish:\n\n* That the conservative closure is the program's actual runtime call graph: candidates and historical choices overapproximate it, and a safe program can be rejected.\n\nReview obligations the rule-coverage map associates with this rule (identifiers of the checker's Residual account):\n\n* R-QUALIFY: the checker's detection is qualified for this invocation, toolchain and capability* R-INVARIANT: the intended invariants, write paths and callers are all covered by proof-bearing interfaces* R-INTENT: the proposition expresses the intended requirement, with its quantifiers, hypotheses and limits\n\nEvery accepted result lists all residual obligations as open, whatever rules it checked: R-INTENT, R-INVARIANT, R-LAWS, R-BOUNDARY, R-NONVACUITY, R-DOC, R-COST, R-QUALIFY (and R-GRAPH for a serialized-graph claim). A listed identifier is an open obligation, never a completed review.\n\nEvery accepted result also relies on these trusted mechanisms (the checker's Trusted account), which no rule verifies:\n\n* toolchain: Lean elaborator, kernel and compiler at the snapshot's toolchain identity* acquisition: manifest parsing, Lake loading, and source, configuration and dependency acquisition* extraction: environment extraction, compiler and worker processes, and JSON transport* runtime: native runtime and every execution boundary reported as trusted\n\n","context":"Regula rule reference\u0009Rule index\u0009RG3001: Execution closure must have no unresolved paths","header":"What a passing result establishes","id":"/rules/RG3001/#RG3001-established"}});